+# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones
+
+## What changed
+
+`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the HR target's lower bound is now `start` instead of `value`.
+
+```diff
+-    return {"units": "%lthr", "value": lo, "end": hi}
++    return {"units": "%lthr", "start": lo, "end": hi}
+```
+
+That is the whole code change, plus a docstring explaining why the key name matters.
+
+## Why
+
+Intervals.icu decides how to export an HR target to the FIT file from the shape of the target:
+
+- `{value, end}` is exported as a **zone-mapped (Lap HR) target**. The watch maps it onto its own HR zones. This is what
+  `readback/watch-notes.md` shows: every step came out as a band from the watch's zone table (LTHR 168: 118–137,
+  151–162, 137–151), never the range we planned. It is also why changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands but they
+  still stopped at zone edges.
+- `{start, end}` is exported as an **instant-HR corridor** in the bpm that Intervals calculates from its own LTHR (172).
+  The watch shows those numbers as they are.
+
+The API accepts both shapes and returns them unchanged, so the readback looked fine. The problem only showed up on the
+watch.
+
+Targets stay as `%lthr` percentages. The earlier attempts in the notes, `units: "bpm"` (214–249 on the watch) and
+`start_bpm`/`end_bpm` (constant 101), are both wrong shapes, so I didn't go back to either.
+
+Expected result for `plans/tempo-run.json` after re-pushing, using Intervals LTHR 172:
+
+| step | % LTHR | watch should show |
+|---|---|---|
+| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | ~117–129 |
+| Tempo | 88–93 | ~151–160 |
+| Float | 75–82 | ~129–141 |
+| Cool-down | 65–72 | ~112–124 |
+
+**Checked:** `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now writes `{"units": "%lthr", "start": …, "end": …}` for all four
+steps, including the steps inside the repeat block.
+
+**Not checked:** I didn't push anything or sync a watch (ground rules: no external services). To confirm, run
+`./push.sh`, sync through Garmin Connect, and compare the watch with the table above. The watch's LTHR (168) differs from
+Intervals' (172), so the check is meaningful: if the numbers match the table, the targets come from Intervals and not
+the watch.
+
+## Which parts of the colleague's note applied
+
+**Applied**
+- The main finding: `{value, end}` gives a zone-mapped target and `{start, end}` gives an instant-HR corridor. This
+  matches our symptom exactly and is the fix.
+- Keep `%lthr` percentages and let Intervals convert them at export. The generator already did this, so I kept it.
+- Their failed attempts (`bpm` giving 214–249, `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` giving a constant 101) match our notes. That
+  confirmed those routes were dead ends.
+- The verification method: compare the watch with %×LTHR from Intervals, using the fact that the two LTHRs differ.
+
+**Not applied**
+- The "bonus trap", meaning a top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar so steps show in the web UI. Nobody
+  reported missing steps in the UI, and our readback has `workout_doc.steps` populated. Their note also says a
+  description the parser can't read makes Intervals wipe both representations. Adding one without being asked would
+  risk breaking a working push. If steps do turn out to be invisible in the Intervals web UI, that is the next thing to
+  look at. Watch their grammar rules there: `m` means minutes, distance is `mtr`/`km`, no digit at the start of a label,
+  no bare `Z2` outside cycling.
+- Their setup and credential steps, and creating or deleting test events. I didn't contact any external service.
